IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA
CASE NO: W.P. (Crl.) No. 167 of 2012
CITATION: AIR 2015 SC 1523
YEAR: 2015

CORAM / BENCH:
Hon'ble Judges: J. Chelameswar, R.F. Nariman

PARTIES:
Shreya Singhal vs Union of India

STATUTES & ACTS REFERRED:
Information Technology Act, 2000, Constitution of India

KEY PROVISIONS & SECTIONS:
Section 66A IT Act, Section 69A IT Act, Article 19(1)(a), Article 19(2)

LEGAL KEYWORDS & TOPICS:
online free speech, unconstitutional, chilling effect, vagueness, intermediary liability

================================================================================
                    JUDGMENT SUMMARY & RATIO DECIDENDI
================================================================================

Struck down Section 66A of the Information Technology Act, 2000 in its entirety for violating Article 19(1)(a). Held that liberty of thought and expression cannot be suppressed on vague assertions of causing annoyance or inconvenience.

1. ISSUES CONSIDERED:
   The court addressed questions of legal interpretation under Section 66A IT Act, Section 69A IT Act, Article 19(1)(a), Article 19(2).

2. ANALYSIS & PRECEDENT CONTEXT:
   Having examined the statutory scheme, fundamental principles of jurisprudence,
   and constitutional safeguards, the Court reiterated the necessity of strict
   compliance with statutory procedure and evidentiary standards.

3. HELD & BINDING RATIO:
   The principle formulated in this judgment constitutes binding judicial precedent
   within its territorial and appellate jurisdiction.

